+    docs(board): split the publish-drift census's two flags so --repo stops being described as the ref resolver
+    
+    docs/BOARD.md said "`--repo <path>` selects the repository resolving the ref; `--no-head`
+    skips ancestry". The first clause is false for the ancestry leg and true only for the
+    published bytes, so a reader who followed it got `FAIL HEAD ancestry` with the ref
+    resolvable.
+    
+    scripts/board_drift.py reads Git from two flags:
+    
+    - `repo` (line 98; `git -C <repo> show <ref>:<file>` at line 111) — published bytes only;
+    - `workdir` (lines 167-172: `rev-parse HEAD`, `rev-parse --verify <ref>^{commit}`,
+      `merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD <ref>`) — the HEAD/ancestry leg, which never sees `--repo`.
+    
+    Measured 2026-11-04 against a workdir fixture holding byte-identical 126-row board files
+    and no `origin/main` ref: the documented `--workdir A --repo <repo-with-ref> --ref origin/main`
+    form prints `FAIL HEAD ancestry — git rev-parse --verify origin/main^{commit}: exit 128` and
+    `RESULT: DRIFT (1 failed)`, and the same run with `--no-head` prints `RESULT: OK`. The doc now
+    states the split, both code locations, the measured failure shape and the `--no-head` route.
+    
+    Evidence: docs/audits/code-factory-docs-2026-11-04-CF-129.md
+    Gates: bash tests/selftest.sh -> SELFTEST: PASS, rc 0, loadavg 9.02
